---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const plugged = assertion('two-thousand-eight-hundred-wells-with-no-plugging-on-file')
const orphan = assertion('a-hundred-and-eight-orphan-wells')
const shape = assertion('the-oil-field-is-a-shape-east-of-the-city')
const depth = assertion('one-formation-at-one-depth')
const late = assertion('the-register-begins-after-the-field-does')

const spec = (a: typeof plugged, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const pluggedSpec = spec(plugged, 'Wells')
const orphanSpec = spec(orphan, 'Wells')
const shapeSpec = spec(shape, 'Wells per square mile')
const depthSpec = spec(depth, 'Wells, and feet')
---

<Article slug="the-wells-that-are-still-there">
  <p>
    The oil strike of 9 May 1885 is the best-dated event in this county's history, and until now
    everything downstream of it was narrative: the population that doubled, the refinery that is
    still running, the city that got its shape. The one thing nobody had counted was the holes. Ohio
    keeps a register of every well it has a record of, and Allen County has four thousand eight
    hundred and forty-nine. Every one of them is vertical, and none is flagged for the
    Utica or the Marcellus: the horizontal drilling that reopened eastern Ohio after 2010 never came
    to this county.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={plugged} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="plugged"
    title="What the register says became of them"
    caption="Wells recorded as having produced carry no date of plugging at all. Of those recorded as plugged and abandoned, 84 carry the date it happened."
    columns={['Status', 'Wells']}
    rows={plugged.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="plugged" set:html={JSON.stringify(pluggedSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    A well that produced and was never plugged is a shaft to a limestone bed a quarter-mile down,
    sitting under a field or a lawn or a road. What the register establishes is not that 2,851 such
    shafts are open — it is that the state has no paper saying they were closed, which is a
    different and smaller claim, and the only one available.
  </p>
  <Gloss>
    Getting even that far took reading the right file. The same agency publishes a map of these wells
    and the register the map is drawn from, and they disagree about 2,933 of the 4,831 rows they
    share. The map calls 2,821 of them <em>Unknown status</em>; the register calls the same 2,821
    <em>Historical Production Well</em>. The map is not lying — it has no symbol for a well that
    produced long ago and was never plugged, so those wells land in the bucket a map keeps for what
    it cannot draw. Read off the map, this county has 2,903 wells nobody knows anything about, which
    is more alarming and less true.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={orphan} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="orphan"
    title="The wells the state has taken responsibility for"
    caption="Allen County's share of Ohio's orphan-well programme: wells with no solvent owner, which the state plugs at public expense. It is 2.2 per cent of the county's wells."
    columns={['Programme status', 'Wells']}
    rows={orphan.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(0)}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="orphan" set:html={JSON.stringify(orphanSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    An orphan well is one with no solvent owner left to plug it, so the state does it. A hundred and
    eight here have been identified, of which eleven have actually been plugged under the programme
    and one was plugged as an emergency. The operator column on the other 4,741 reads
    <em>HISTORIC OWNER</em>, which is the register's way of saying that whoever drilled the well is
    not a company any more.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the field was</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={shape} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="shape"
    title="Wells per square mile"
    caption="Every wellhead coordinate placed in a township by point-in-polygon. All 4,849 fell inside one, and the thirteen areas sum to 402.5 square miles against the gazetteer's 402.545."
    columns={['Township', 'Per sq mi']}
    rows={shape.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(2)}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="shape" set:html={JSON.stringify(shapeSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The field is not the county. It is a band running north and east of Lima, thick under Bath and
    Perry and effectively absent from Auglaize Township in the south-east corner, eight wells across
    thirty-six square miles. Lima itself has 206, fifteen to the square mile under a city — drilled
    when most of that ground was still farm.
  </p>
  <p>
    That band is the reason for everything else on this page. It is not a fact about who owned what
    or who drilled first; it is a fact about where one limestone bed comes close enough to the
    surface to be worth reaching, and the towns, the refinery and the railway spurs were arranged
    around it afterwards.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={depth} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="depth"
    title="One bed, one depth"
    caption="Of the 290 wells recording a total depth, four in five fall in a two-hundred-foot band. That band is the Trenton Limestone, which is what the Lima–Indiana field was."
    columns={['Measure', 'Value']}
    rows={depth.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="depth" set:html={JSON.stringify(depthSpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>What the register cannot see</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={late} />
  <p>
    Regulation arrived after the industry did. A permit date exists for 3.2 per cent of these wells,
    a completion date for 2.2 and a plug date for 3.4, and the earliest completion in the county is
    1890 — five years after the strike, by which time the field was already national news. So the
    boom is countable in what it left and not in what it did: four thousand eight hundred and
    forty-nine holes, of which the state can say when thirty of them were finished.
  </p>
</Article>
